1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,423
The CTO asks: "Which part can you bring
back, which part can you not, and why?"

2
00:00:09,763 --> 00:00:14,384
That question has an exact answer, and
it has three parts, because "two days of

3
00:00:14,424 --> 00:00:18,928
work" is three different kinds of thing
to Git. The committed part comes back

4
00:00:18,968 --> 00:00:22,705
through the reflog, Git's local
list of where each ref has been.

5
00:00:22,745 --> 00:00:27,367
The staged part survives as a blob, an
object that holds the bytes of one file,

6
00:00:27,407 --> 00:00:32,324
without a name. And the part that was never
staged is gone, because Git never stored

7
00:00:32,364 --> 00:00:32,501
it.

8
00:00:32,841 --> 00:00:36,166
Today you prove each of those
three sentences in a terminal.

9
00:00:36,206 --> 00:00:40,821
Count them off as we go. And then you learn
the reset that would have refused to do

10
00:00:40,861 --> 00:00:41,438
the damage.

11
00:00:43,278 --> 00:00:46,868
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:00:46,908 --> 00:00:51,629
git reset is the command most people mean
when they say "undo", and the one most

13
00:00:51,669 --> 00:00:55,498
people fear. Both reactions come
from not knowing what it writes.

14
00:00:55,838 --> 00:01:01,390
The plan. First the three modes, --soft,
--mixed and --hard, on one file that has a

15
00:01:01,430 --> 00:01:06,982
different version in each of the three
trees, so that you can see how far each mode

16
00:01:07,022 --> 00:01:12,439
spreads. The three trees are your files
on disk, the index, which is the proposed

17
00:01:12,479 --> 00:01:17,492
next commit, and HEAD, the commit you're
on. Then what happens inside .git,

18
00:01:17,532 --> 00:01:20,995
including ORIG_HEAD, a safety
net with a hole in it.

19
00:01:21,335 --> 00:01:27,142
Then section 11.5: four kinds of work,
one hard reset, and what is left of each.

20
00:01:27,182 --> 00:01:32,404
Then the two modes that check before
they overwrite, --keep and --merge.

21
00:01:32,444 --> 00:01:38,104
And last, git commit --amend, which turns
out to be a soft reset followed by a

22
00:01:38,144 --> 00:01:38,616
commit.

23
00:01:38,956 --> 00:01:43,444
All of it applies to private history only.
That was the deciding question of the

24
00:01:43,484 --> 00:01:44,067
last video.

25
00:01:45,907 --> 00:01:47,319
After this video you can:

26
00:01:47,659 --> 00:01:54,573
Predict the working tree, the index and the
branch after --soft, --mixed and --hard.

27
00:01:54,913 --> 00:02:00,001
Sort work into committed, staged and never
staged, and say what a hard reset leaves

28
00:02:00,041 --> 00:02:01,299
recoverable for each.

29
00:02:01,639 --> 00:02:04,926
Preview a hard reset and set
a way back before running it.

30
00:02:05,266 --> 00:02:10,312
Choose --keep over --hard, and
explain what --merge protects.

31
00:02:10,652 --> 00:02:13,484
Describe git commit --amend as a
soft reset followed by a commit.

32
00:02:15,324 --> 00:02:20,301
In one sentence: git reset <commit> 🟡
CAUTION makes the current branch point at

33
00:02:20,341 --> 00:02:25,636
<commit>, and its mode decides whether the
index, with --mixed, the default, and the

34
00:02:25,676 --> 00:02:28,812
working tree, with --hard,
are rewritten to match.

35
00:02:29,152 --> 00:02:33,778
Precisely. With a commit argument and
no path, reset does up to three things.

36
00:02:34,118 --> 00:02:39,238
One: it writes the current commit ID to
ORIG_HEAD and makes the branch that HEAD

37
00:02:39,278 --> 00:02:42,269
points at name <commit>.
--soft does only this.

38
00:02:42,609 --> 00:02:47,350
Two: it rewrites the index to match
<commit>. --mixed does this as well.

39
00:02:47,690 --> 00:02:52,510
Three: it rewrites the tracked files
in the working tree to match <commit>.

40
00:02:52,550 --> 00:02:53,936
Only --hard does this.

41
00:02:54,276 --> 00:02:59,493
<commit> defaults to HEAD. So git reset
alone unstages everything, and git reset

42
00:02:59,533 --> 00:03:03,700
--hard alone discards every
uncommitted change to tracked files.

43
00:03:03,740 --> 00:03:09,089
On a detached HEAD, where HEAD holds a
commit ID instead of a branch name, there's

44
00:03:09,129 --> 00:03:12,243
no branch to move, and
HEAD itself is rewritten.

45
00:03:12,583 --> 00:03:17,360
Inside .git: the file HEAD doesn't change.
It still says ref: refs/heads/main.

46
00:03:17,400 --> 00:03:21,867
What changes is the content of that
ref. ORIG_HEAD holds the previous ID.

47
00:03:21,907 --> 00:03:25,016
Both reflogs gain a line.
And no object is deleted.

48
00:03:25,356 --> 00:03:29,231
Now why --hard is 🔴. --soft and
--mixed never write a file.

49
00:03:29,271 --> 00:03:34,739
--hard overwrites files, and what that costs
depends on one question for each piece

50
00:03:34,779 --> 00:03:39,915
of work: did Git ever store it as an
object? Git writes a blob when content is

51
00:03:39,955 --> 00:03:45,223
staged or committed. An edit that was only
saved in the editor was never stored.

52
00:03:45,563 --> 00:03:49,979
Quick quiz. You stage a file,
and a hard reset takes it away.

53
00:03:50,019 --> 00:03:55,970
Where is its content? A, in the reflog. B,
in the object database, without a name.

54
00:03:56,010 --> 00:03:57,723
C, nowhere. Your answer?

55
00:04:02,563 --> 00:04:06,182
B. Staging wrote the blob, and
a reset deletes no object.

56
00:04:06,222 --> 00:04:07,338
The demo finds it.

57
00:04:07,678 --> 00:04:13,168
How long do the recoverable kinds last?
The textbook states the defaults from the

58
00:04:13,208 --> 00:04:18,904
manual. A reflog entry for a commit that
its branch no longer reaches is kept for 30

59
00:04:18,944 --> 00:04:24,094
days, gc.reflogExpireUnreachable. And
garbage collection removes unreachable

60
00:04:24,134 --> 00:04:29,420
objects, the ones nothing reaches any
more, once they're older than two weeks,

61
00:04:29,460 --> 00:04:34,678
gc.pruneExpire. The lab configuration
overrides the reflog expiry, as video 1

62
00:04:34,718 --> 00:04:35,360
explained.

63
00:04:35,700 --> 00:04:40,536
The alternatives. git reset --keep <commit>
🟡 moves the branch like --hard, carries

64
00:04:40,576 --> 00:04:45,412
your uncommitted changes across, and refuses
to run when it would have to overwrite

65
00:04:45,452 --> 00:04:50,111
one of them. git reset --merge <commit>
🔴 does the same for unstaged changes and

66
00:04:50,151 --> 00:04:54,987
throws staged changes away. It exists for
backing out of a merge, where whatever is

67
00:04:55,027 --> 00:04:57,160
staged is merge output and should go.

68
00:04:57,500 --> 00:05:02,931
And git commit --amend 🟡 replaces the last
commit with a new commit that has the

69
00:05:02,971 --> 00:05:08,334
same parent and is built from the current
index. Git's manual calls it "a rough

70
00:05:08,374 --> 00:05:13,873
equivalent" of git reset --soft HEAD^,
some changes, and git commit -c ORIG_HEAD.

71
00:05:15,713 --> 00:05:20,636
A picture helps. The textbook's
analogy: a bookmark in a lab notebook.

72
00:05:20,676 --> 00:05:26,592
--soft moves the bookmark back. --mixed also
clears the page you had drafted for the

73
00:05:26,632 --> 00:05:29,428
next entry. --hard also clears the desk.

74
00:05:29,768 --> 00:05:33,034
The analogy breaks in a useful
way: no page is torn out.

75
00:05:33,074 --> 00:05:37,993
The entries after the bookmark stay in the
notebook, and Git discards unmarked pages

76
00:05:38,033 --> 00:05:38,997
only after weeks.

77
00:05:39,337 --> 00:05:44,264
So two things to hold. First, reset always
starts by moving the bookmark, and the

78
00:05:44,304 --> 00:05:49,292
mode only decides how far the change spreads.
Second, the notebook is safe and the

79
00:05:49,332 --> 00:05:54,320
desk isn't. What was written into the
notebook, committed or even only staged, can

80
00:05:54,360 --> 00:05:57,631
be found again. What was only
lying on the desk can't.

81
00:05:59,471 --> 00:06:05,370
Now with real commits. Before: three in
a row, with main on the last, 9d940ca.

82
00:06:05,710 --> 00:06:10,215
After git reset HEAD~1, in any
mode, main is one commit back.

83
00:06:10,255 --> 00:06:16,100
9d940ca has no branch, but three names
remain: ORIG_HEAD, and entry one in both

84
00:06:16,140 --> 00:06:16,696
reflogs.

85
00:06:17,036 --> 00:06:19,925
And here's the table you'll
fill in during the demo.

86
00:06:19,965 --> 00:06:24,374
The first row is the starting state. The
other three rows are yours to predict.

87
00:06:26,214 --> 00:06:34,751
That's the starting state: 0.70 in
HEAD, 0.80 in the index, 0.90 on disk.

88
00:06:35,091 --> 00:06:40,865
The three modes. For each, predict the
three values and the status letters before

89
00:06:40,905 --> 00:06:44,813
the output. First --soft. Say
them out loud. I'll wait.

90
00:06:49,653 --> 00:06:55,228
The log lost a commit, and HEAD now
holds 0.60. The index and the file are

91
00:06:55,268 --> 00:07:01,450
untouched. The status is still MM, but it's
now measured against the older commit:

92
00:07:01,490 --> 00:07:06,762
git commit at this point would record
0.80 directly on top of d44696e.

93
00:07:06,802 --> 00:07:09,114
Next, --mixed. Predict the row.

94
00:07:13,954 --> 00:07:20,051
The index now equals the new HEAD, and
Git reports what is left as unstaged.

95
00:07:20,091 --> 00:07:24,088
The file still says 0.90.
And now --hard. Predict.

96
00:07:28,928 --> 00:07:36,528
All three places hold 0.60. The staged
0.80 and the unstaged 0.90 are gone from

97
00:07:36,568 --> 00:07:38,945
every place you can name.

98
00:07:39,285 --> 00:07:40,298
Inside .git.

99
00:07:40,638 --> 00:07:47,007
HEAD says ref: refs/heads/main before and
after. The ref file changed from the ID

100
00:07:47,047 --> 00:07:53,496
starting 9d940ca to the one starting d44696e.
ORIG_HEAD holds the previous ID, and

101
00:07:53,536 --> 00:07:57,690
both reflogs gained the line
reset: moving to HEAD~1.

102
00:07:57,730 --> 00:08:01,409
Commit 9d940ca is still
in the object database.

103
00:08:01,749 --> 00:08:04,682
ORIG_HEAD. Resetting to
it undoes the reset.

104
00:08:05,022 --> 00:08:10,972
But look at the last line. The undo was
itself a reset, so it overwrote ORIG_HEAD,

105
00:08:11,012 --> 00:08:12,725
which now names d44696e.

106
00:08:13,065 --> 00:08:18,418
ORIG_HEAD is one slot, not a history. And
more commands write it than the manual

107
00:08:18,458 --> 00:08:23,946
lists. labs/run ch11/orig-head-slot: a
merge followed by a stash, the command that

108
00:08:23,986 --> 00:08:28,597
parks uncommitted work. Predict what
ORIG_HEAD names after the stash.

109
00:08:33,437 --> 00:08:38,219
After the merge, ORIG_HEAD named
5522f41, the commit to go back to.

110
00:08:38,259 --> 00:08:43,833
But git stash push ends with an internal
reset to HEAD, and every reset writes

111
00:08:43,873 --> 00:08:48,151
ORIG_HEAD. So it now names the
merge commit itself, 26ec8ae.

112
00:08:48,191 --> 00:08:52,325
git reset --hard ORIG_HEAD
would leave the merge in place.

113
00:08:52,365 --> 00:08:56,212
The rule: use ORIG_HEAD only
as the very next command.

114
00:08:56,252 --> 00:09:01,034
After anything else, read the reflog,
which has the whole sequence.

115
00:09:01,374 --> 00:09:07,378
Why --hard is 🔴. git reset --hard 🔴
DANGEROUS. The five answers before it runs.

116
00:09:07,718 --> 00:09:12,721
What it changes: the branch ref, the index,
and every tracked file that differs from

117
00:09:12,761 --> 00:09:13,801
the target commit.

118
00:09:14,141 --> 00:09:18,952
What it can destroy: every uncommitted
change to tracked files, and untracked files

119
00:09:18,992 --> 00:09:23,861
that are in the target's way. Untracked
means the file has no entry in the index and

120
00:09:23,901 --> 00:09:24,680
isn't ignored.

121
00:09:25,020 --> 00:09:30,542
How to preview: three read-only commands.
labs/run ch11/reset-hard-preview.

122
00:09:30,882 --> 00:09:35,308
First, the commits that will leave the
branch. Second, the uncommitted work, by

123
00:09:35,348 --> 00:09:39,095
kind. Third, the tracked content
that differs from the last commit.

124
00:09:39,435 --> 00:09:45,396
How to recover: commits through the reflog
or ORIG_HEAD, staged content through git

125
00:09:45,436 --> 00:09:51,108
fsck --lost-found, nothing else at all.
git fsck is the checker that lists what

126
00:09:51,148 --> 00:09:52,265
nothing reaches.

127
00:09:52,605 --> 00:09:57,506
When it's appropriate: when git status is
clean, or after you've read git diff HEAD

128
00:09:57,546 --> 00:10:02,446
and want none of it. Otherwise turn the
work into objects first, with two commands.

129
00:10:07,786 --> 00:10:12,051
The stash entry holds the uncommitted
work, and the branch holds the commits.

130
00:10:12,391 --> 00:10:17,146
Now without the safety net.
labs/run ch11/reset-hard-proofs.

131
00:10:17,186 --> 00:10:20,102
Four kinds of work in one repository.

132
00:10:20,442 --> 00:10:25,774
The committed change e574174.
sweep.yaml, staged and never committed.

133
00:10:25,814 --> 00:10:30,756
The edit to metrics.txt, never
staged. And notes.txt, untracked.

134
00:10:30,796 --> 00:10:35,971
Try it now, thirty seconds, on
paper: write those four in a column.

135
00:10:36,011 --> 00:10:41,887
Next to each, predict what is left after
git reset --hard HEAD~1, and where.

136
00:10:46,727 --> 00:10:58,259
The branch is back at 67b1722, metrics.txt
says 0.71 again, and sweep.yaml is no

137
00:10:58,299 --> 00:11:04,913
longer on disk. Only
notes.txt was left alone.

138
00:11:05,253 --> 00:11:10,310
Kind one, committed work: the reflog has
it. That's the CTO's first sentence.

139
00:11:10,650 --> 00:11:15,441
Kind two, staged work: an object without
a name. git add wrote the content as a

140
00:11:15,481 --> 00:11:20,394
blob. The hard reset removed the index
entry, the only thing that referred to it.

141
00:11:20,734 --> 00:11:25,855
A dangling blob, acc57c7, and git cat-file
-p prints the sweep configuration.

142
00:11:25,895 --> 00:11:29,676
Dangling means nothing refers
to it. The content is back.

143
00:11:29,716 --> 00:11:35,038
The file name isn't. A blob stores content
only, and the name lived in the index

144
00:11:35,078 --> 00:11:40,400
entry. After a reset with ten staged files,
you get ten anonymous blobs, and you

145
00:11:40,440 --> 00:11:45,226
identify them by reading them. That's
the second sentence, and the quiz.

146
00:11:45,566 --> 00:11:51,062
Kind three, never-staged work: nothing to
find. git hash-object --stdin computes the

147
00:11:51,102 --> 00:11:53,566
ID that a piece of content would have.

148
00:11:53,906 --> 00:11:57,188
For the staged content the
object exists: type blob.

149
00:11:57,228 --> 00:12:02,427
For the never-staged edit, Git computes
the ID it would have had, and git cat-file

150
00:12:02,467 --> 00:12:07,155
fails: no such object was ever written.
That's the third sentence, proved.

151
00:12:07,495 --> 00:12:12,733
Here's that loss as a root-cause box. The
lost version existed only in the working

152
00:12:12,773 --> 00:12:17,753
tree, and the hard reset overwrote the
file. There's no fix inside Git, so the

153
00:12:17,793 --> 00:12:21,872
prevention is the last line: commit
or stash first, or use keep.

154
00:12:22,212 --> 00:12:25,334
Kind four, untracked files:
untouched, with one exception.

155
00:12:25,374 --> 00:12:28,278
An untracked file whose path
the target commit tracks.

156
00:12:28,618 --> 00:12:34,155
--keep refused. --hard overwrote the
file without a word: two days of tuning

157
00:12:34,195 --> 00:12:40,099
replaced by alpha: 0.7. The manual admits
it in one clause: --hard "may overwrite

158
00:12:40,139 --> 00:12:45,749
untracked files". And there it is: --keep,
the reset that would have refused.

159
00:12:46,089 --> 00:12:50,932
--keep and --merge. labs/run
ch11/reset-keep-merge.

160
00:12:50,972 --> 00:12:57,825
One commit to drop, a staged change
in serve.yaml, an unstaged change in

161
00:12:57,865 --> 00:12:58,974
metrics.txt.

162
00:13:03,814 --> 00:13:07,728
Both edits survived --keep,
and both are now unstaged.

163
00:13:08,068 --> 00:13:14,649
After --merge, the unstaged edit to metrics.txt
is still there, and the staged batch

164
00:13:14,689 --> 00:13:21,191
size of 64 is gone: the file says 32 again.
--merge earns its 🔴 through staged work

165
00:13:21,231 --> 00:13:24,107
only. Preview with git diff --cached.

166
00:13:24,447 --> 00:13:29,567
--hard from the same state loses both. And
when the local change is in a file that

167
00:13:29,607 --> 00:13:31,581
the dropped commit also changed:

168
00:13:31,921 --> 00:13:34,782
--keep stops, and the branch didn't move.

169
00:13:35,122 --> 00:13:39,846
The rule that follows: to drop
commits, type --keep, not --hard.

170
00:13:39,886 --> 00:13:46,099
On a clean tree the two do the same thing.
On a dirty tree, --keep carries your work

171
00:13:46,139 --> 00:13:48,332
across, or stops and says why.

172
00:13:48,672 --> 00:13:55,206
Amend. labs/run ch11/amend-is-soft-reset.
A line was forgotten in the last commit.

173
00:13:55,546 --> 00:14:02,441
The reflog says commit (amend). Old commit
d59fe4c, new commit ab64793, same parent

174
00:14:02,481 --> 00:14:04,446
9a98a06, different tree.

175
00:14:04,786 --> 00:14:09,859
As a graph: two commits on one parent. main
names the new one, and no branch names

176
00:14:09,899 --> 00:14:12,353
the old one. Now the same thing by hand.

177
00:14:12,693 --> 00:14:18,139
Both routes end with a commit whose parent
is 9a98a06 and whose tree is b35836e.

178
00:14:18,179 --> 00:14:23,008
The two commit IDs differ only because
the committer timestamps differ.

179
00:14:23,048 --> 00:14:28,220
An amend is therefore a rewrite of
exactly one commit: private history only.

180
00:14:28,560 --> 00:14:31,286
One difference matters when
you want the old commit back.

181
00:14:31,626 --> 00:14:37,813
--amend doesn't write ORIG_HEAD. The previous
commit is one step back in the reflog,

182
00:14:37,853 --> 00:14:38,480
HEAD@{1}.

183
00:14:40,320 --> 00:14:41,968
Five mistakes to watch for.

184
00:14:42,308 --> 00:14:45,732
Typing --hard to drop a commit
while the tree is dirty.

185
00:14:45,772 --> 00:14:50,895
Root cause: --hard is defined as "make the
working tree match the commit", and the

186
00:14:50,935 --> 00:14:53,792
working tree is the one
place without history.

187
00:14:54,132 --> 00:14:59,318
Running git reset --hard ORIG_HEAD some
commands after the operation you want to

188
00:14:59,358 --> 00:15:04,413
undo. Root cause: ORIG_HEAD is one slot
that every reset writes, including the

189
00:15:04,453 --> 00:15:07,287
internal reset at the
end of git stash push.

190
00:15:07,627 --> 00:15:11,194
Assuming untracked files are
always safe from a hard reset.

191
00:15:11,234 --> 00:15:16,267
Root cause: an untracked file at a path the
target commit tracks is in the target's

192
00:15:16,307 --> 00:15:17,673
way and is overwritten.

193
00:15:18,013 --> 00:15:22,447
Expecting git fsck --lost-found
to return files with their names.

194
00:15:22,487 --> 00:15:28,092
Root cause: a blob stores content only;
the name lived in the index entry that the

195
00:15:28,132 --> 00:15:29,055
reset removed.

196
00:15:29,395 --> 00:15:33,909
Amending a commit that has been pushed.
Root cause: an amend creates a new commit

197
00:15:33,949 --> 00:15:38,631
with the same parent, which is a rewrite of
one commit and diverges from what others

198
00:15:38,671 --> 00:15:38,912
have.

199
00:15:40,752 --> 00:15:45,747
Now, out of the lab. Two example uses, for
a team that ships a retrieval service,

200
00:15:45,787 --> 00:15:47,239
both on private history.

201
00:15:47,579 --> 00:15:52,208
Three commits named "wip", "wip 2"
and "fix typo" before a review.

202
00:15:52,248 --> 00:15:57,657
git reset --soft @{u} moves the branch
back to what is pushed, and leaves the

203
00:15:57,697 --> 00:16:00,770
combined change staged
for one clean commit.

204
00:16:01,110 --> 00:16:04,693
A commit that mixes a refactoring
with a behavior change.

205
00:16:04,733 --> 00:16:09,714
git reset HEAD~1, the mixed default,
leaves both in the working tree, unstaged,

206
00:16:09,754 --> 00:16:11,875
ready to be split with git add -p.

207
00:16:12,215 --> 00:16:17,249
And the team rule that comes out of section
11.6: in runbooks and in muscle memory,

208
00:16:17,289 --> 00:16:20,488
the command for dropping
commits is git reset --keep.

209
00:16:20,528 --> 00:16:25,501
The day somebody has two days of tuning in
an untracked file, that one word is the

210
00:16:25,541 --> 00:16:28,679
difference between an error
message and an incident.

